                <h2>1. Standardize the Measurement Framework</h2>
<p>Overseas advertising fund management should not rely on account balances alone. Divide key metrics into four categories and assign an owner, alert threshold, and response deadline to each one.</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Metric</th>
<th>Calculation</th>
<th>Operational Purpose</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Available balance ratio</td>
<td>Available balance ÷ planned daily spend</td>
<td>Determines whether a top-up is required</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Budget utilization</td>
<td>Actual spend ÷ planned budget</td>
<td>Detects overspending or underdelivery</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Recharge processing time</td>
<td>Funding received − payment submitted</td>
<td>Measures capital turnover efficiency</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Billing variance</td>
<td>Platform invoice − internal ledger</td>
<td>Identifies missing records, fees, or FX differences</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Also track daily account limits, account health, currency, time zone, legal paying entity, and ownership. Thresholds should reflect actual payment processing times and spending patterns rather than a one-size-fits-all rule.</p>
<h2>2. Daily SOP: Follow These Seven Steps</h2>
<h3>Step 1: Maintain an Account and Funding Ledger</h3>
<p>Create a master list for every advertising platform, including platform name, account ID, currency, time zone, owner, budget, balance, and recharge history. In multi-account teams, adopt a consistent naming format such as region—product—currency—account number. Update owners and permissions whenever staff or business responsibilities change.</p>
<h3>Step 2: Check Budgets Against Planned Spend</h3>
<p>Before campaigns start, compare yesterday’s actual spend, remaining budgets, and upcoming large campaigns. Review accounts with unexpectedly low budget utilization. The cause may be rejected creatives, limited delivery, or an account restriction—not simply an unused budget.</p>
<h3>Step 3. Apply Tiered Balance Alerts</h3>
<p>Classify accounts as normal, watch, and critical. Move an account into watch status when its balance covers less than half a day of planned spending. Mark it critical when it cannot cover the next major debit cycle. Consider pending recharges, credit limits, holiday processing delays, and automatic debit rules. A submitted payment is not necessarily usable funding until it has cleared.</p>
<h3>Step 4. Verify Every Recharge</h3>
<p>Before submitting a payment, confirm the amount, currency, paying entity, destination account, and expected processing time. Teams with multiple entities or regions should require dual approval rather than acting on a chat screenshot alone. After submission, record the payment time, receipt time, transaction number, fee, and exchange rate, then verify the credited account in the platform.</p>
<h3>Step 5. Allocate Funds Across Accounts</h3>
<p>When funding is tight, prioritize accounts with validated conversion paths that are still inside an effective campaign period. Do not distribute balances evenly by default. Before transferring funds, check account health, creative approvals, payment methods, and spending limits. Maintain separate emergency reserves to cover delayed recharges and sudden increases in spend.</p>
<h3>Step 6. Reconcile Invoices on a Fixed Schedule</h3>
<p>Compare live spend with platform invoices daily. Reconcile recharge transactions, platform fees, taxes, exchange rates, and internal payment records every week. Investigate each variance by billing date, debit date, cross-day attribution, and refund status. Keep unmatched transactions in a pending reconciliation list until the invoice is confirmed; do not count them as available funds.</p>
<h3>Step 7. Document and Close Exceptions</h3>
<p>Every alert should end with a documented outcome, such as funded, paused, budget adjusted, or awaiting invoice confirmation. Preserve screenshots, platform records, and operator details for audit and review.</p>
<h2>3. Weekly Review: Focus on Trends</h2>
<p>Produce three reports: budget utilization, recharge and settlement status, and billing variances. Review whether available funding is declining, whether recharge approvals are creating delays, whether spend is overly concentrated in one account, and whether old discrepancies remain unresolved.</p>
<p>For example, an anonymized cross-border e-commerce team separated payment approval, recharge execution, and invoice confirmation across different roles. This made it easier to compare submitted payments with funds actually credited to the correct accounts. The goal is not artificial zero variance; every difference should have an owner, due date, and resolution status.</p>
<p>The resulting workflow is continuous: planned budgets determine funding needs, balance alerts trigger recharge review, payment records update available funding, invoices close the reconciliation cycle, and weekly metrics inform the next budget period.</p>
